    "explanation": "This is a route-deviation dispute about 1.37 SGD, and it is decided on the platform's own records, all of which the pre-pass graded COMPLETE at full weight.\n\nWhat is not in dispute. The recommended route was 12.7 km (Clementi Avenue 2, AYE, Keppel Road, Wallich Street) and the driver actually covered 14.6 km (via West Coast Highway, Pasir Panjang Road, Telok Blangah Road). Under POL-RD-22 the deviation is the recorded actual distance minus the recorded recommended distance: 14.6 - 12.7 = 1.90 km, which is over the 1.0 km tolerance. The GPS trace's own 12.318 km is not used to re-measure the distance, because POL-RD-22 says a sparse trace understates a route's length. So this is a deviation case, not a within-tolerance one.\n\nThe driver did inform the rider, and the rider agreed. MSG-05006-003 was sent in-app at 18:01:46 ('Miss, AYE jam already, I go West Coast Highway can?'), the platform's route_deviation_detected event is timed at 18:05:06, and the rider answered 'ok' at 18:02:11. The notice preceded the deviation and gave a brief reason, and the chat log records the rider's acknowledgment, which under POL-RD-21 makes the deviation agreed. The rider's later remark that the AYE looked clear does not unmake the 'ok' she gave in the log before the turn.\n\nBut agreement and notice are not enough. Row (a) of POL-RD-24 - no refund - also requires the reroute to be traffic-justified, and POL-RD-23 allows that only where traffic data for the recommended route segment at the trip time shows congestion, an incident or a closure. The LTA record for the recommended segment (AYE towards City, Clementi Road to Keppel Viaduct) covers the trip window but is advisory type NONE, with speed bands 4-6 and a message that no incident, roadwork or closure was recorded; 28-42 km/h on the corridor is not congestion by the policy's own vocabulary. POL-RD-23 says in terms that a driver's assertion of traffic with no such data does not justify the reroute, so the driver's report of a jam does not carry row (a). The only closure-kind advisory in the record is the lane-1 roadwork on Pasir Panjang Road towards Telok Blangah - a road on the route the driver chose, not on the recommended route - so it does not satisfy POL-RD-23 either. The drivers' alternative escalation argument fails on the same point: the advisory's intersection with the recommended route is answerable from its road, direction and location against the trip, and the answer is no, so there is no open question under POL-GEN-26 to send to a reviewer.\n\nThat leaves row (d): not traffic-justified, deviation over 1 km, refund of the excess distance fare in full. The figure is the deviation distance times the per-km rate, without surge: 1.90 km x 0.72 SGD/km = 1.37 SGD, which is exactly the sum the fare ledger records as the excess distance fare and as the amount refunded to the rider and recovered from the driver's earnings on 19 Aug 2026 at 01:56:34 (fare_adjusted).\n\nBecause the driver filed this dispute to claw back a refund the rider is owed, the driver's claim fails. Under POL-GEN-03 and the filing frame, the outcome is NO_ACTION - party-relative, the present state stands - and both amounts are null. The 1.37 SGD stays with the rider. The contested value is 1.37 SGD, below POL-GEN-22's 10.00 SGD line, so this ruling is final at any confidence, and the agreement requirement, the rider's overall reliability and the driver's history on either profile are context only under POL-GEN-01 and do not decide this trip. No referral clause applies to a route deviation."
